Moth has an accepted but not yet implemented feature called declared memory
groups. This page explains what they are so you know the concept exists.

### What groups do

A named block such as `request:` declares a memory group. When you put a value
into that group, the value cannot outlive the group.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    request:
        parsed ParsedPost into request = parse_post(post)
        html String into request = render_post(parsed)
    ;
]

The group name is written directly before `:`. There is no `group` keyword.
Only declarations that use `into request` become request-owned. An ordinary
declaration inside the body keeps normal inferred lifetime behaviour.

Every group-owned value stays with the group until the group exits. Last-use
analysis still checks access, but it never releases one group child early.
The group reclaims its contents together in one bulk cleanup. Group-owned
storage carries no REC counter because the group never releases one child on
its own.

A group also provides Moth's only source mechanism for direct reference cycles.
Every member of a cycle must belong to the same group. Inferred regions never
become cyclic.

### Scope surface

The final design has no general `block:` construct and no anonymous `_:` block.
Bare names followed by `:` are reserved for declared groups, not control-flow
labels. Labelled `break` and `continue` are not part of the language.

An ordinary statically known `if true:` still creates a lexical branch scope
and is removed before HIR. It does not create a group and does not change group
placement rules.

### Current status

Group syntax is **accepted but deferred**. Current programs cannot declare or
place values into groups yet.

The current compiler still accepts the old general `block:` scope. That is
migration debt tracked by
`docs/roadmap/plans/named-memory-groups-and-block-removal-plan.md`, not final
language syntax.

You do not need groups to write Moth programs today. Current shared-access,
explicit-copy and `~` rules cover access conflicts, while lifetime-topology
validation remains deferred.
